@section(Coastal Protection Hazard)

The coastal protection hazard module assesses the role of ecosystems in risk reduction, as determined by hazard (coastal flood hazard), exposure, and vulnerability. Specifically, risk results “from dynamic interactions between climate-related hazards with the exposure and vulnerability of the affected human or ecological system to the hazards” (Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change 2022). In this module there have been novel efforts in linking the ecosystem services dimension and the local population risk dimension using an accounting in building a framework (La Notte, et. al 2025) that introduces ecosystems into a population based systematic accounting structure aligned with the SEEA-EA framework. The model quantifies the population benefiting from reduced hazard exposure by comparing hazard levels with and without ecosystem protection, focusing on populations and areas prone to coastal flooding. This module produces spatially explicit indicators of hazard proneness, exposure, vulnerability, and avoided risk.
